<h1>Node.js Crypto Hashing Guide for Files and Text</h1>
<p>Node.js includes a built-in crypto module that can calculate hashes, create HMACs, derive keys, and perform other cryptographic operations. For checksums and general hashing, the common method is createHash. It supports algorithms such as SHA-256 and, where available, legacy algorithms such as MD5. The right algorithm depends on the job.</p>
<p>The official <a href="https://nodejs.org/api/crypto.html">Node.js crypto documentation</a> describes crypto.createHash as the method used to create hash instances. A basic text hash uses createHash('sha256'), update(data), and digest('hex'). For MD5 compatibility, the structure is similar, but MD5 should be limited to old integrations and non-security checks.</p>
<p>For files, avoid reading very large files into memory all at once. Use streams. Create a read stream, pipe or feed chunks into the hash object, and read the digest when the stream ends. This approach works better for release files, media archives, logs, and large backups. It also fits Node's event-driven style.</p>
<p>A common use case is release automation. A build script can calculate SHA-256 checksums for downloadable files and write them into a JSON manifest or text file. Users can compare those values with local calculations on Windows, macOS, or Linux. For a site that publishes tools or downloads, this is a simple trust-building habit.</p>
<p>Do not use createHash('sha256') as your complete password storage solution. SHA-256 is useful, but it is fast. Password storage should use a password hashing library for bcrypt, Argon2id, scrypt, or PBKDF2 with proper parameters. Node's crypto module includes scrypt and pbkdf2 functions, but application teams often use framework-level libraries to reduce mistakes.</p>
<p>Be careful with encoding. Hashing the string café as UTF-8 is not the same as hashing a different byte representation. When comparing hashes across systems, confirm that every system uses the same encoding and line endings. Text copied from a webpage can also include hidden spaces or different newline characters.</p>
<p>For summer development work, hashing scripts are useful for package checks, build artifacts, exported reports, and migration validation. A Node script can compare old and new files after a pipeline change or confirm that a generated archive matches a published manifest. That is a practical use of hashing outside pure security.</p>
<p>For production scripts, handle errors explicitly. A file stream can fail because the path is wrong, permissions are missing, or the file changes while being read. Hashing code that silently returns an empty digest or ignores stream errors creates false confidence. Verification tools should fail loudly and tell the user what went wrong.</p>
<p>For APIs, distinguish between hashing and HMAC. A plain hash verifies data integrity only when both sides already trust the input source. An HMAC uses a secret key and is often better for message authentication. Do not replace an HMAC with a plain SHA-256 hash in signed API workflows.</p>
<p>Use SHA-256 for new checksums, MD5 only for compatibility, and dedicated password hashing for passwords. If you are choosing between MD5 and SHA-256, read <a href="/md5-vs-sha256.html">MD5 vs SHA-256</a>. If you are working on passwords, start with <a href="/password-hashing-vs-encryption.html">Password Hashing vs Encryption</a> before writing code.</p>
